      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The recovery of left ventricular function (LVF) after revascularization takes time. alpha-Adrenergic blockade acutely improves coronary blood flow and LVF, whereas the effects of more prolonged alpha-adrenergic blockade on LVF recovery after stenting are unknown."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In 32 patients (age 58 +/- 12 y) with an 82% +/- 6% stenosis, ejection fraction (EF) and systolic thickening (%Th) were measured by transthoracic echocardiography before and 30 minutes to 2 hours after revascularization. In a double-blinded protocol, either 200 microg/kg urapidil or placebo was given intravenously, and LVF was measured 10 minutes later. Two hours later, oral treatment with 30 mg/d drug or placebo was started, and LVF measured again after 24 hours and 3 months."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Before revascularization, EF was 49.4% +/- 8.5% (+/-SD) and 51.3% +/- 8.8% in the urapidil-treated and the placebo groups, respectively. Thirty minutes to 2 hours after coronary stenting, EF was unchanged. After intravenous drug administration, EF increased to 56.5% +/- 9.7%). At 24 hours and 3 months after revascularization, EF became 59.5% +/- 7.9% and 59.6% +/- 8.2% in the urapidil-treated group, respectively, whereas EF in the placebo group did not change (50.4% +/- 5.7% and 49.7% +/- 4.9%, respectively). Revascularization did not acutely improve %Th. Intravenous urapidil improved %Th from 31.4% +/- 17.6% to 44.2% +/- 11.6%, whereas there was no change in the placebo group. At 3 months, %Th was 49.5% +/- 12.9% in the urapidil-treated group and 39.7% +/- 8.9% in the placebo group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These data suggest that long-term alpha-adrenergic blockade might improve LVF at midterm after coronary revascularization."
        }
      ]},
